{
  "kind": "tea",
  "teaId": "widow-oil",
  "text": "The creditors were coming for her sons and all she had was one jar of oil.\n\nSecond Kings four, opening scene. A widow cries out to Elisha and lays out the situation. Her husband is dead. He feared the Lord. And the creditor is coming to take her two sons to be his slaves.\n\nNot the furniture. The children. That's the debt system she's trapped in.\n\nAnd Elisha asks a question I think about a lot. \"What shall I do for thee? Tell me, what hast thou in the house?\"\n\nAnd she says, \"Thine handmaid hath not any thing in the house, save a pot of oil.\"\n\nNothing. Except one thing.\n\nAnd he says go borrow vessels from all your neighbours. Empty ones. And then, and this is the line, \"Borrow not a few.\"\n\nSo she does. Shuts the door on herself and her sons, and starts pouring.\n\nAnd it keeps coming. Vessel after vessel. Until she says to her son, \"Bring me yet a vessel.\" And he says, \"There is not a vessel more.\"\n\nAnd the oil stayed.\n\nRead that again. The supply stopped exactly when the containers did.\n\nShe sells it, pays the debt, and lives on the rest.",
